From 5dbc1f2a79e6e7855926739fedd754ccc615c07a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 14:40:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Harden API authorization, tenancy, and SSRF controls

Adds a resolved caller identity and makes it the basis for every authorization
decision, replacing the self-asserted X-Auricrux-Email header. Once Auth:Enabled
is on the header is ignored outright, so a caller can no longer present another
account's email to inherit its plan, quota, or stored data.

- Default-deny API gate: a global filter denies unauthenticated calls when
  authentication is enabled. Endpoints opt out with [AuricruxPublic], so a newly
  added endpoint is protected by omission rather than exposed by it. Previously
  only /api/secure was gated, leaving chat, agent, browse, vision, media,
  workspace, memory, and account anonymous even with auth turned on.
- Plan changes are fail-closed: an upgrade now requires the billing provisioning
  key or an administrator identity. POST /api/account/{email}/upgrade previously
  let any anonymous caller move any account to pro-plus.
- Workspace and conversation memory are partitioned per caller, so a path or
  session id from one account cannot address another's files or transcript.
- SSRF guard resolves the target host and requires every resolved address to be
  public unicast, covering IPv6 and IPv4-mapped addresses; redirects are followed
  manually and revalidated per hop. The previous hostname string blocklist was
  bypassable by any DNS name pointing at link-local space.
- Content fetched from the web is delimited and defanged before entering a prompt,
  so a page cannot issue instructions to the agent that drives further tool calls.
- Rate limiting partitions by account with a client-address fallback and a global
  backstop; forwarded headers are honoured only from configured proxies.
- Correlation IDs from clients are sanitized before reaching logs and headers.
- The interaction map is bounded and evicted oldest-first instead of growing for
  the lifetime of the process.

Tests: 61 passing. The upgrade test asserted the anonymous-upgrade behavior, so it
is replaced by coverage of the provisioned path plus regressions for the refusals.
